  <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-indexing">
    <p class="section-title">Phase 1 — Tree Index Generation</p>

```
<div class="flow-section">
  <div class="flow-title">
    <div class="dot" style="background:#4fffb0;box-shadow:0 0 8px #4fffb0;"></div>
    How PageIndex Builds the Document Tree
  </div>
  <p class="flow-subtitle">Source files: <code style="color:#5c7dff;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:12px;">pageindex/page_index.py</code> (PDF) &amp; <code style="color:#5c7dff;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:12px;">pageindex/page_index_md.py</code> (Markdown)</p>
</div>

<div class="pipeline">

  <div class="step phase-index">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">01</div>
      <div class="step-line"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">Document Ingestion</div>
        <div class="step-tag">Input Layer</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">The pipeline accepts a PDF or Markdown file. For PDFs, <code style="color:#4fffb0;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;">pymupdf</code> and <code style="color:#4fffb0;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;">PyPDF2</code> are used to extract raw page-level text. Each page's text is stored in a list indexed by page number. For Markdown, <code style="color:#4fffb0;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;">#</code> headers determine the hierarchy level.</p>
      <div class="step-detail">
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Entry point</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val"><code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#5c7dff;">run_pageindex.py</code> — parses CLI args, calls <code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#5c7dff;">pdf_to_tree()</code> or <code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#5c7dff;">md_to_tree()</code></span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">PDF libraries</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">pymupdf (fitz), PyPDF2 — extract text per page, preserving order</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Config params</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val"><code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#ffd166;">--toc-check-pages 20</code> · <code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#ffd166;">--max-pages-per-node 10</code> · <code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#ffd166;">--max-tokens-per-node 20000</code></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step phase-index">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">02</div>
      <div class="step-line"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">Table of Contents Detection</div>
        <div class="step-tag">Structure Parsing</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">The first N pages (default: 20) are scanned for an existing Table of Contents. If a TOC is found, its hierarchical structure is parsed to define the tree skeleton — chapters and section boundaries become tree nodes. If no TOC exists, the LLM generates a structural outline by analyzing page content patterns.</p>
      <div class="step-detail">
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">PDF TOC</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Scans first 20 pages for "Contents", "Index", or similar patterns. Extracts page numbers for section boundaries.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Markdown TOC</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Header level (#, ##, ###) directly maps to tree depth. <code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#ffd166;">#</code> = root, <code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#ffd166;">##</code> = L2, etc.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">LLM fallback</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">When no TOC is found, GPT-4o analyzes the first pages to infer logical section structure and generate TOC headings.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step phase-index">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">03</div>
      <div class="step-line"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">Hierarchical Node Construction</div>
        <div class="step-tag">Tree Building</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">Each section from the TOC becomes a tree node. If a section exceeds <code style="color:#ffd166;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;">max_pages_per_node</code> (10 pages) or <code style="color:#ffd166;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;">max_tokens_per_node</code> (20,000 tokens), it is recursively split into child nodes. This ensures every node fits within the LLM's effective reasoning window while preserving natural semantic boundaries.</p>
      <div class="step-detail">
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">node_id</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Sequential zero-padded string ("0001", "0002"…) — unique retrieval key for raw content lookup</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">start_index / end_index</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">0-indexed page range (exclusive end). Enables precise raw text extraction at query time.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">nodes (children)</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Array of child Node objects — recursive nesting creates the full tree. Leaves have empty <code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#ffd166;">nodes</code> array.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step phase-index">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">04</div>
      <div class="step-line"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">LLM Node Summarization</div>
        <div class="step-tag">Enrichment</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">For each node, the raw page content in its range is passed to GPT-4o to generate a concise summary. These summaries serve as the retrieval "hints" — they allow the LLM at query time to reason about relevance without reading the full raw content. Doc-level descriptions are also generated when <code style="color:#ffd166;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;">--if-add-doc-description yes</code>.</p>
      <div class="step-detail">
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">summary field</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">2-4 sentences capturing key facts, topics, named entities — optimized for LLM reasoning, not human reading</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">prefix_summary</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Some nodes carry context from their parent to make summaries self-contained for retrieval</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Toggle flags</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val"><code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#ffd166;">--if-add-node-summary yes/no</code> · <code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#ffd166;">--if-add-doc-description yes/no</code></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step phase-index">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">05</div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">JSON Tree Output</div>
        <div class="step-tag">Storage</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">The complete tree is serialized to a JSON file. This becomes the persistent "index" — loaded into LLM context at query time. The JSON is human-readable, debuggable, and fully self-describing. No database or embedding infrastructure required.</p>
      <div class="code-block">
```

<span class="c"># Example output structure</span>
{
<span class="k">“doc_description”</span>: <span class="s">“Federal Reserve Annual Report 2023…”</span>,
<span class="k">“nodes”</span>: [
{
<span class="k">“title”</span>: <span class="s">“Financial Stability”</span>,
<span class="k">“node_id”</span>: <span class="s">“0006”</span>,
<span class="k">“start_index”</span>: <span class="v">21</span>,
<span class="k">“end_index”</span>: <span class="v">22</span>,
<span class="k">“summary”</span>: <span class="s">“The Federal Reserve monitors…”</span>,
<span class="k">“nodes”</span>: [
{
<span class="k">“title”</span>: <span class="s">“Monitoring Financial Vulnerabilities”</span>,
<span class="k">“node_id”</span>: <span class="s">“0007”</span>,
<span class="k">“start_index”</span>: <span class="v">22</span>,
<span class="k">“end_index”</span>: <span class="v">28</span>,
<span class="k">“summary”</span>: <span class="s">“The Federal Reserve’s monitoring…”</span>
}
]
}
]
}
</div>
</div>
</div>

```
</div>

