  <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-retrieval">
    <p class="section-title">Phase 2 — Reasoning-Based Query Retrieval</p>

```
<div class="flow-section">
  <div class="flow-title">
    <div class="dot" style="background:#5c7dff;box-shadow:0 0 8px #5c7dff;"></div>
    How PageIndex Finds Relevant Nodes for Your Query
  </div>
  <p class="flow-subtitle">The LLM navigates the tree index like a human expert — no cosine similarity, no embeddings, pure reasoning.</p>
</div>

<div class="pipeline">

  <div class="step phase-retrieval">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">01</div>
      <div class="step-line"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">User Query Received</div>
        <div class="step-tag">Query Input</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">A natural language question arrives. Unlike vector RAG which immediately embeds it, PageIndex does <em>nothing</em> to the query at this point — it will be passed verbatim to the LLM along with the tree structure. Chat history (if any) is also preserved to enable multi-turn contextual reasoning.</p>
      <div class="step-detail">
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Example query</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">"What was the total value of deferred assets held by Reserve Banks in 2023?"</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Multi-turn context</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Prior conversation history is included — if user asked about "financial assets" before, the retriever knows to stay in that domain</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step phase-retrieval">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">02</div>
      <div class="step-line"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">Tree JSON Injected into LLM Context</div>
        <div class="step-tag">In-Context Index</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">The entire tree JSON (titles, node_ids, summaries, page ranges) is loaded directly into the LLM's context window. This is called the <strong style="color:#5c7dff;">in-context index</strong> — the LLM "reads" the document's Table of Contents in one pass, just like a human flipping to the front pages.</p>
      <div class="step-detail">
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">What's included</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">All node titles, summaries, node_ids, and page ranges for the entire document tree (NO raw page content yet)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Why no raw text</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Raw content of all pages would exceed context limits. The tree serves as a compact, reasoning-friendly map of the document.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step phase-retrieval">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">03</div>
      <div class="step-line"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">LLM Node Selection via Tree Reasoning</div>
        <div class="step-tag">Core Intelligence</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">The LLM is prompted to reason over the tree structure and return a <code style="color:#ffd166;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;">node_list</code> of relevant node_ids. It explicitly classifies each section as relevant or irrelevant based on query intent — not surface similarity. The LLM's "thinking" field makes this fully traceable.</p>
      <div class="step-detail">
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Prompt template</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val"><em>"You are given a question and a tree structure. Find all nodes likely to contain the answer. Return JSON with 'thinking' and 'node_list'."</em></span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">LLM reasoning</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">e.g., "Deferred assets → likely in financial statements section or Appendix G statistical tables → check nodes 0021, 0031"</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Cross-references</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">LLM follows "see Appendix G" hints in summaries — navigates to that node automatically, unlike vector search</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Multi-node</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Returns a list — multiple nodes may be selected if the answer requires synthesizing across sections</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step phase-retrieval">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">04</div>
      <div class="step-line"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">Raw Content Extraction from Selected Nodes</div>
        <div class="step-tag">Content Fetch</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">Using the selected <code style="color:#ffd166;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;">node_ids</code>, the system looks up the corresponding page ranges (<code style="color:#ffd166;font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;">start_index → end_index</code>) and extracts the raw text from those PDF pages. Only these specific pages are loaded — targeted extraction, not the full document. For vision-based pipelines, page images are extracted instead of text.</p>
      <div class="step-detail">
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Lookup mechanism</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val"><code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#5c7dff;">node_id → (start_index, end_index) → raw text pages</code></span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Coherence preserved</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Full natural sections retrieved — no mid-sentence cuts like fixed-chunk RAG. Context integrity intact.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Vision RAG variant</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Page images (not text) are extracted and passed to a VLM — handles complex tables, charts, grids natively</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step phase-retrieval">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">05</div>
      <div class="step-line"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">Sufficiency Check (Agentic Loop)</div>
        <div class="step-tag">Agentic Decision</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">In the agentic retrieval mode, the LLM evaluates whether the extracted content is <em>sufficient</em> to answer the query. If not, it loops back — selecting additional nodes or drilling into sub-nodes. This iterative refinement mimics how a human re-reads sections when the first try doesn't yield the answer.</p>
      <div class="step-detail">
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Sufficient → </span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Proceed to answer generation with accumulated context</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Not sufficient → </span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Loop: expand node selection, fetch neighboring nodes, or check child nodes for more detail</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step phase-answer">
    <div class="step-connector">
      <div class="step-num">06</div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-label">Answer Generation with Grounded Context</div>
        <div class="step-tag">Final Output</div>
      </div>
      <p class="step-desc">The retrieved raw content from selected nodes is assembled and passed to the LLM alongside the original query. The answer is generated grounded in the specific pages, with full traceability — the response can cite exact section titles and page numbers. This is the final context window: <strong style="color:#c084fc;">Query + Retrieved Node Content → Answer</strong>.</p>
      <div class="step-detail">
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Context assembly</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val"><code style="font-family:Space Mono;font-size:11px;color:#c084fc;">prompt = f"Question: {query}\n\nContext:\n{retrieved_text}\n\nAnswer:"</code></span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Traceability</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Each retrieved node's title and page range is known — answer can cite "Section 6.1, pages 22-28"</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-detail-row">
          <span class="step-detail-key">Streaming</span>
          <span class="step-detail-val">Retrieval happens inline during reasoning — TTFT (time-to-first-token) is not blocked by a retrieval gate</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

