    <p class="section-title">PageIndex vs Traditional Vector RAG</p>

```
<table class="compare-table">
  <tr>
    <th>Dimension</th>
    <th>Vector-based RAG</th>
    <th>PageIndex (Reasoning-based)</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Document Preparation</td>
    <td><span class="tag-bad">chunk</span> Fixed-size text splits (512–1000 tokens) breaking semantic boundaries</td>
    <td><span class="tag-good">natural</span> Hierarchical sections from real document structure — chapters, pages, logical units</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Index Type</td>
    <td><span class="tag-bad">external</span> Vector database (Pinecone, Chroma, FAISS) with embedding vectors</td>
    <td><span class="tag-good">in-context</span> JSON tree structure loaded directly into LLM context — no DB infra</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Retrieval Method</td>
    <td><span class="tag-bad">similarity</span> Cosine similarity / approximate nearest neighbor search</td>
    <td><span class="tag-good">reasoning</span> LLM explicitly reasons: "Where is this answer most likely located?" → returns node_ids</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Query Understanding</td>
    <td>Query intent → embedding vector → surface semantic match</td>
    <td>Query intent → LLM reasoning → navigation decision → structural match</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Cross-References</td>
    <td><span class="tag-bad">fails</span> "See Appendix G" — doesn't follow internal links; Appendix text not similar to query</td>
    <td><span class="tag-good">follows</span> LLM reads "see Appendix G" in a summary and navigates there in the next loop</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Chat History</td>
    <td><span class="tag-bad">isolated</span> Each query embedded independently — no memory of prior turns</td>
    <td><span class="tag-good">contextual</span> Full conversation history in prompt — LLM adjusts retrieval based on prior Q&amp;A</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Explainability</td>
    <td><span class="tag-bad">opaque</span> "Top-k most similar chunks" — no rationale for why chunks were selected</td>
    <td><span class="tag-good">traceable</span> LLM's "thinking" field explains exactly why each node was selected</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Infrastructure</td>
    <td>Embedding model + vector DB + index maintenance + synchronization</td>
    <td><span class="tag-good">simple</span> Just a JSON file + LLM API. Update a section → re-index only that subtree</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Domain Expertise</td>
    <td>Embedding similarity misses domain-specific relevance (e.g., tax table vs tax discussion)</td>
    <td><span class="tag-good">expert</span> LLM applies domain knowledge: "financial liabilities are in balance sheet, not MD&amp;A"</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Accuracy (FinanceBench)</td>
    <td><span class="tag-bad">lower</span> Typical vector RAG: 60–85% on complex financial docs</td>
    <td><span class="tag-good">98.7%</span> State-of-the-art — Mafin 2.5 powered by PageIndex</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Semantic Integrity</td>
    <td><span class="tag-bad">fragmented</span> Fixed chunks split sentences, break tables, sever context</td>
    <td><span class="tag-good">coherent</span> Full natural sections retrieved — tables and paragraphs stay intact</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Best For</td>
    <td>Short texts, semantic similarity search, product recommendations, "vibe retrieval"</td>
    <td>Long professional documents: financial reports, legal filings, technical manuals, academic texts</td>
  </tr>
</table>

